 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to the transfer of patient\nmedical records upon the closure of a health care provider's office",
    "basePrintNo": "S5367",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5367-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 11,
       "fullName": "John Liu",
       "imgName": "1045_John_Liu.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1225,
       "sessionMemberId": 1223,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "LIU"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §18, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5367\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nthe transfer of patient medical records upon the closure of a health\ncare provider's office\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill establishes requirement for orderly transfer of medical\nrecords, if a physician closes an office, this bill makes it clear that\nfailure to comply with law regarding patient access to his or her own\nrecords would be in violation of what is generally accepted reasonable\nexpectation, an expectation to control the information about an individ-\nual's own health, and in the event a patient is not granted access to\ntheir medical records the health commissioner would be empowered to take\nemergency action to require new tests if the failure to release records\ntimely poses a health threat to individual patients.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill adds paragraph (j) to subdivision 1 of § 18 of\nthe public health law to define \"cease to do business in this state.\"\n\nSection two of the bill adds subdivision 13 to § 18 of the public health\nlaw. Paragraph (a) requires that when a health care provider that has\npossession of patient information has decided to permanently cease to do\nbusiness in this state, that thirty days prior to taking such action the\nhealth care provider make a good faith effort to notify their current\npatients of the impending closure and of the patient's right to request\ntheir patient information or medical records be sent to the health care\nprovider, facility, or practitioner of their choice or returned to the\npatient.\n\nParagraph (b) affirms that the provisions of this subdivision shall not\naffect any of the rights provided pursuant to section seventeen which\nconcerns the release of medical records.\n\nParagraph (c) provides that nothing in this section shall affect the\ntime period that a health care provider is lawfully required to retain a\npatient's information and medical records.\n\nParagraph (d) states that the provisions of this subdivision shall apply\nonly to patients whose chart include the patient's written permission to\nbe notified.\n\nSection three of the bill provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen a patient visits their doctor, the patient is the consumer of the\ndoctor's services and when such a doctor determines to no longer contin-\nue their practice, his or her patients have a reasonable expectation the\ninformation and medical records that the doctor has compiled over the\ncourse of taking care of the patient will be available to the patient or\nbe transferred in accordance with the patient's wishes.\n\nHowever, obtaining access to that information can be difficult after a\nprovider has ceased operations. Although health care providers in New\nYork State are required to keep medical records for a minimum of six\nyears, trying to locate contact information from a provider who has\nclosed their practice can be burdensome.\n\nThis legislation would make it less likely that New Yorkers will have to\nencounter such difficulties by requiring that they be sent a notifica-\ntion that their health care provider intends to cease to do business and\nabout their right to request that their patient information or medical\nrecords be sent to the healthcare provider, facility, or practitioner of\ntheir choice or that those records be returned to them. This legislation\nalso addresses patient privacy concerns by requiring that a patient\nconsent to have the aforementioned notification sent to them. In receiv-\ning this notification, New Yorkers will be provided the opportunity to\nmake a determination as to how they want their medical records managed.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-2018: A.6847-A - Referred to Health\n2015-2016: A.6677 - Referred to Health\n2013-2014: A.8080-A - Referred to Health\n2012: A.1076 1 - Referred to Health\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nIt is not anticipated that this legislation will have any significant\nfiscal impact on the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
